19.1  Dynamic linking and ldd

TL;DR
  - Most programs don't contain the libraries they use: at start-up, the dynamic
    loader finds the .so files they need and loads them.
  - ldd program lists those libraries and where each was found. "not found"
    marks the one that's missing.
  - The loader searches LD_LIBRARY_PATH first, then the folders ldconfig knows
    (from /etc/ld.so.conf.d/), then the standard ones.

      ldd /usr/bin/curl
      ldconfig -p | grep libssl

On your machine
  PATH      /home/alice/.local/bin
            /usr/local/bin (doesn't exist)
            /usr/bin
            . ⚠ the current folder, wherever you are
  Repeated  /usr/bin (listed more than once: harmless, the first one wins)
  Libraries LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/opt/oracle/lib
  Runs      programs built for x86-64, glibc

Pitfalls
  - "error while loading shared libraries: libfoo.so.1": install the package
    that provides it, rather than copying or linking another version.
  - After putting a library in a new folder, list that folder in a file in
    /etc/ld.so.conf.d/, then run sudo ldconfig.
  - Don't run ldd on a program you don't trust: it may run the program's code.
    objdump -p program | grep NEEDED only reads it.
  - LD_LIBRARY_PATH set for everything breaks other programs: set it only for
    the command that needs it.

Deep dive

  ▾ Why link dynamically?

    One copy of a library serves every program, on disk and in RAM, and a
    security fix in the library fixes all of them at once. Static linking puts
    everything in the program: easier to copy to another machine, but bigger,
    and every program must be rebuilt to get a fix. Go programs are often linked
    that way.

  ▾ What is the number in libfoo.so.1?

    The major version, part of the library's "soname". A program asks for
    libfoo.so.1; the file is often libfoo.so.1.2.3, and libfoo.so.1 is a link to
    it. A new major version (.so.2) means an incompatible change, and it can be
    installed next to the old one.

Related
  Commands  ldd, ldconfig, objdump
  Paths     /etc/ld.so.conf.d/
  See also  idk 19.2 ELF and "command not found"
  See also  idk 17.4 syscalls and strace
